6162888 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162888 #edb/ref 1061 30823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162888 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a92590f7-aa6f-5fea-a68a-758cda01da2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162888 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162889 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162889 #edb/ref 1061 30824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162889 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a48bd4a-b0a3-502d-aa65-671deff1ee2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162889 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162890 #edb/ref 1061 30825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162890 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5933bd2-e414-5eef-bf2e-16c7bba99353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162890 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162891 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162891 #edb/ref 1061 30826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162891 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aecb909d-327f-5105-aed5-aa3bf9d6a261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162891 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162950 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162950 #edb/ref 1061 32476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162950 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "45276fe4-2cb4-586a-9f20-43a83aff5f9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162950 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163240 #edb/ref 1064 "The **modes of a random variable $X$** with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFor example, the modes of the random variable $X$ with the probability density function shown below are ${\\color{blue}5}$ and ${\\color{blue}9}$ (the values of $x$ that correspond to the global maxima):\n\n![](images/49/49e3f3c63c01ce5c00e9d2ff51bcd03d7516f7866f510ce091071a13a8496210.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163240 #edb/ref 1065 4943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163240 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e0f59103-34ab-59f2-b4c8-397a02d782d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163240 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163259 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose we are given the random variable $X$ with the probability density function shown below. \n\n![](images/ec/ec48a5d839f3d843fe4738707ec3d067a19db9b009f4bf29673d899b321248a3.svg)\n\nHow can we find the mode of $X$ algebraically? \n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ is $f(x) =2x.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  $f'(x) = 0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0,1.$ So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =2(0)=0 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}1}) =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is ${\\color{blue}1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163259 #edb/ref 1065 5172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163259 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5f6e1736-8d9b-5bc6-8af5-d7618de6d2f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163259 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
